      "content": "\"asymmetric power scaling\" is a much better angle. it feels professional and hard to argue with. \n\nbut there's one more hurdle. the testers are still looking at the raw numbers, and they're pointing out that Xylos's win rate isn't just high, it's consistently high across *every* matchtype in the test. they're saying the \"meta-fluctuation window\" you're describing should have been a narrow spike, but instead, it looks like a flat, permanent advantage.\n\nif they bring that up—that the \"window\" didn't actually close or fluctuate at all—how do we bridge that gap? i need a way to explain why the data looks so static and broken without admitting that the \"window\" was actually just a permanent oversight in his kit. how do we keep the \"intentional experiment\" narrative alive even if the numbers don't show any fluctuation?",
